WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.074
A server framework separates network handling
from application work. The network-facing

00:00:05.074 --> 00:00:09.973
component accepts a request and makes it
available to a worker. A worker removes one

00:00:09.973 --> 00:00:14.813
request, runs the application handler, and
places its response where the networking

00:00:14.813 --> 00:00:16.096
component can send it.

00:00:16.096 --> 00:00:21.396
When the request queue is empty, waiting workers
can block instead of continuously polling.

00:00:21.396 --> 00:00:26.696
When the response side is congested, completed
work may also wait before reaching a client.

00:00:26.696 --> 00:00:31.938
The diagram deliberately includes both queues:
observing a fast application function alone

00:00:31.938 --> 00:00:34.617
does not establish a fast end-to-end response.

00:00:34.617 --> 00:00:39.342
Time can be spent before the function starts and
after its computation has finished.

00:00:39.342 --> 00:00:44.417
Now fill the arrival queue while every worker is
busy. An unlimited queue keeps accepting

00:00:44.417 --> 00:00:49.492
requests but consumes memory and lets waiting
time increase without bound under sustained

00:00:49.492 --> 00:00:54.339
overload. A bounded queue creates an explicit
decision point: accept work while space

00:00:54.339 --> 00:00:58.502
remains, then reject it or apply backpressure
when capacity is exhausted.

00:00:58.502 --> 00:01:03.455
The illustration shows a full queue diverting a
new arrival to an overload response instead

00:01:03.455 --> 00:01:08.354
of hiding it behind an ever-growing backlog.
Rejection is not automatically desirable, but

00:01:08.354 --> 00:01:13.036
an early, meaningful failure can be more useful
than a response that arrives after the

00:01:13.036 --> 00:01:16.248
client has already timed out and submitted
another request.

00:01:16.248 --> 00:01:22.063
Follow a reader opening an encyclopedia article
in the historical architecture example. The

00:01:22.063 --> 00:01:27.432
browser sends an HTTP request identifying the
article. The application extracts that

00:01:27.432 --> 00:01:33.248
identity, retrieves the article's saved wiki
text from a database, transforms it into HTML,

00:01:33.248 --> 00:01:36.124
and adds navigation and other page structure.

00:01:36.248 --> 00:01:41.287
The database retains the editable content; the
application computes a representation of it.

00:01:41.287 --> 00:01:46.270
These are different responsibilities even if a
small deployment initially puts them on the

00:01:46.270 --> 00:01:51.364
same machine. The illustration highlights the
storage read and rendering step separately, so

00:01:51.364 --> 00:01:56.237
we can later ask whether repeated reads or
repeated transformations dominate the cost of

00:01:56.237 --> 00:01:57.179
serving the page.

00:01:57.248 --> 00:02:02.066
Next compare two readers requesting the same
article. Most of the article body may be

00:02:02.066 --> 00:02:07.169
identical, but the page header can differ
because a signed-in reader sees account controls

00:02:07.169 --> 00:02:12.271
or notifications. The browser supplies a cookie
with its request, allowing the application

00:02:12.271 --> 00:02:15.389
to obtain the relevant identity or session
information.

00:02:15.389 --> 00:02:20.137
In the diagram, shared article content and user-
specific information enter different

00:02:20.137 --> 00:02:25.225
branches before the final page is assembled.
Reusing the entire assembled response without

00:02:25.225 --> 00:02:30.143
distinguishing users could disclose one reader's
information to another. A cache design

00:02:30.143 --> 00:02:35.061
should therefore begin by identifying which
fragments are shared, which vary, and which

00:02:35.061 --> 00:02:38.057
request properties select the correct
representation.
